  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (though Tuesday)...\n\nThe ridge over the Intermountain West will continue to be the\ndominant player for our region through the majority of the work\nweek. This means mostly weak winds, warm to hot temperatures, and\nthe potential for monsoonal moisture. High temperatures will\ncontinue a slow steady ramp up into Friday where a lot of the CWA\nwill see highs in the low 90s and potentially low 100s again. Low\nlevel winds will remain out of the west / downslope through the \nweek keeping RH values low, with the lowest values coming late \nThursday and Friday with the higher temps. \n\nAlthough the ridge will dominate the weather across the region, we\nwill be close enough to the apex of the feature that we will be\nimpacted yet another vort max that pushes over the ridge this\nafternoon. Similar to yesterday, another small feature will push \nup out of the Snake River Plain into SW Montana. This will give\nall of our mountains another chance for afternoon showers and \nstorms, as well as the immediate foothills. \n\nThursday will come with the chance for some slightly more\nimpactful weather. A lobe of energy moving out of the PNW will\npush through ID and western MT before quickly warping back into\nthe larger low over BC. This will be enough though to initiate\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms across central MT. There \ndoes not appear to be much of a severe risk with this event, \nhowever inverted-v soundings with dry low levels could result in \nstrong winds out of any storm. In far SE Montana there could be \nsome more traditional severe storms in an area of better CAPE, \nshear, and moisture, but this will be confined to just Carter co. \nMT.\n\nThursday may also come with some locally elevated fire weather\nconcerns. This is forecast to be the hottest day of the stretch\nwith highs in the mid to upper 90s across the CWA. RH values\nshould not be concerningly low since there will be some low level\nmoisture advection out of the Great Plains due to a weak thermal \nlow / borad-scale upslope flow. This will keep min RH values in \nthe teens for much of the area with even the 25% ensemble \nsolutions keeping values above 10%. The surface winds are not \nforecast to be too strong with models showing sustained values in \nthe teens mph with the potential for some localized gusts over 20 \nmph. There will also be showers and storms around in the evening, \nnearly all of which should come with precip. All this to say, \nthere will be elevated fire weather but as of now I do not expect \ncritical conditions. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, that low over off the Pacific coast\nwill be pushed inland and through our area. This will bring \ncooler temperatures and a chance for widespread precipitation. \nThis setup looks like it may also bring some winds due to the \nprogressin and orientation of the low, however the modeled low \nlevel surface winds are not too strong as of now. After this \nweekend's low, the long range models keep the pattern fairly \nactive and progressive through the first week of September. WMR",
